          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Think and Reflect"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.152,
            0.872,
            0.21
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Why does a negative times a negative equal a positive? Think of it in terms of action and debt. If a negative number represents a debt, then multiplying by a negative number represents the removal of that debt."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.213,
            0.872,
            0.272
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "("
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Hint:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " If someone takes away (–) four of your debts that are each worth `3 (that is, –3), you are effectively `12 richer! Therefore, (–3) × (– 4) = +12.)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.374,
            0.31,
            0.622,
            0.34
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Exercise Set 3.2"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.352,
            0.885,
            0.412
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "1. The temperature in the high-altitude desert of Ladakh is recorded as "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "4   ^ { \\circ } \\mathsf { C }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " at noon. By midnight, it drops by "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "1 5 \\; ^ { \\circ } \\mathrm { C } ,"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " What is the midnight temperature?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.415,
            0.886,
            0.497
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2. A spice trader takes a loan (debt) of `850. The next day, he makes a profit (fortune) of `1,200. The following week, he incurs a loss of `450. Write this sequence as an equation using integers and calculate his final financial standing."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.132,
            0.499,
            0.717,
            0.521
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3. Calculate the following using Brahmagupta’s laws:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.181,
            0.526,
            0.326,
            0.548
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(i) "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "( - 1 2 ) \\times 5"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.548,
            0.526,
            0.709,
            0.548
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(ii) "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "(-8) \\times (-7)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.167,
            0.56,
            0.325,
            0.582
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(iii) 0 – (–14)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.548,
            0.561,
            0.7,
            0.582
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(iv) "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "(-20) \\div \\; 4"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.591,
            0.886,
            0.652
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "4. Explain, using a real-world example of debt, why subtracting a negative number is the same as adding a positive number (e.g., 10 – (–5) = 15)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.675,
            0.787,
            0.723
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3.4 Filling the Spaces: Fractions and Rational Numbers"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.734,
            0.886,
            0.811
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "As society grew more complex, measuring became just as important as counting. If a farmer divides a field of wheat among his three children, how much does each get? If a recipe calls for half a cup of ghee, how do we represent that mathematically?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.81,
            0.884,
            0.851
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Numbers that represent parts of a whole are called "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "fractions",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". Just as every natural number has an additive inverse (3 has –3, 19 has –19, etc.),"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.855,
            0.885,
            0.913
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "we can conceive of additive inverses for every positive fraction: "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "-\\frac{3}{4}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " for "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "{ \\frac { 3 } { 4 } }   ,   -   { \\frac { 1 9 } { 7 } }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " for , etc. Let us refer to such additive inverses of positive "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\frac { 1 9 } { 7 }"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
